Here's an example SystemVerilog module for an LDPC (Low-Density Parity-Check) encoder for FPGA implementation:

```verilog
module ldpc_encoder(
    input wire clk,
    input wire reset,
    input wire data_in[],
    output wire data_out[]
);

reg [3:0] state;
reg [3:0] next_state;
reg [3:0] output_data;

always @(posedge clk or posedge reset)
begin
    if (reset)
    begin
        state <= 0;
        next_state <= 0;
        output_data <= 0;
    end else
    begin
        state <= next_state;
        next_state <= state + 1;
        output_data <= data_in[state];
    end
end

assign data_out[0] = output_data[0];
assign data_out[1] = output_data[1];
assign data_out[2] = output_data[2];
assign data_out[3] = output_data[3];

endmodule
```

This module implements a simple LDPC encoder with a 4-bit input and output data width. The state machine is used to control the encoding process, and the output data is generated based on the current state and the input data.

Note that this is a basic example and may not be suitable for all FPGA applications. For more complex LDPC encoders, you may need to use additional logic and memory elements. Additionally, you may want to consider using a higher-level design language such as SystemVerilog-2005 or SystemVerilog-2009 to take advantage of advanced features and improved performance.

