To calculate the distance between two points on the Earth's surface considering it as an ellipsoid, you can use the following MATLAB code:

```matlab
function distance = earth_distance(lat1, lon1, lat2, lon2)
    % Define the Earth's semi-major axis (a) and semi-minor axis (b)
    a = 6378137; % meters
    b = 6356752.3142; % meters

    % Convert latitudes and longitudes from degrees to radians
    lat1 = deg2rad(lat1);
    lon1 = deg2rad(lon1);
    lat2 = deg2rad(lat2);
    lon2 = deg2rad(lon2);

    % Calculate the Earth's flattening (f) and eccentricity squared (e2)
    f = (a - b) / a;
    e2 = 2 * f - f^2;

    % Calculate the radius of curvature in the prime vertical at each point
    N1 = a / sqrt(1 - e2 * sin(lat1)^2);
    N2 = a / sqrt(1 - e2 * sin(lat2)^2);

    % Calculate the Cartesian coordinates of each point
    x1 = (N1 + h1) * cos(lat1) * cos(lon1);
    y1 = (N1 + h1) * cos(lat1) * sin(lon1);
    z1 = ((1 - e2) * N1 + h1) * sin(lat1);
    x2 = (N2 + h2) * cos(lat2) * cos(lon2);
    y2 = (N2 + h2) * cos(lat2) * sin(lon2);
    z2 = ((1 - e2) * N2 + h2) * sin(lat2);

    % Calculate the distance between the two points
    distance = sqrt((x1 - x2)^2 + (y1 - y2)^2 + (z1 - z2)^2);
end
```

