Yes, you can achieve this using SQL database by creating a stored procedure or a trigger that checks for the elapsed time and triggers a break reminder message. However, keep in mind that SQL is primarily used for data manipulation and retrieval, and it might not be the best choice for creating a continuous reminder application.

Here's an example of a stored procedure that checks for the elapsed time and prints a break reminder message:

```sql
CREATE PROCEDURE check_break_time()
BEGIN
    DECLARE start_time TIMESTAMP;
    DECLARE end_time TIMESTAMP;

    SELECT start_time, end_time INTO start_time, end_time FROM work_sessions WHERE id = (SELECT id FROM work_sessions ORDER BY start_time DESC LIMIT 1);

    IF DATEDIFF(MINUTE, start_time, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP) >= 10 THEN
        PRINT 'It has been 10 minutes since you started working. Take a break!';
    END IF;
END;
```

To call this stored procedure, you can use the following SQL statement:

```sql
CALL check_break_time();
```

This will check for the elapsed time and print a break reminder message if 10 minutes have passed since the start_time. However, this approach requires manual execution, and it might not be suitable for a continuous reminder application.

If you want a more suitable solution, you can consider using a programming language like Python or JavaScript, which can interact with your SQL database and provide a more flexible and continuous reminder functionality. These languages offer various libraries and frameworks that can help you create a user interface, schedule tasks, and handle timers.

