Yes, if the server is setting `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` to `*`, it may prevent you from including an authorization header in your request. This is because the `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header controls which origins are allowed to access the resource, and when it is set to `*`, it allows requests from any origin.

To include an authorization header in your request, you can use a pre-flight request (OPTIONS method) to check if the server supports it. If the server supports it, you can include the authorization header in your subsequent requests. Here's an example of how you can do this using JavaScript:
```javascript
// Send a pre-flight request to check if the server supports authorization headers
fetch('https://example.com', {
  method: 'OPTIONS',
  headers: {
    'Authorization': 'Bearer your_access_token',
    'Access-Control-Request-Method': 'GET',
    'Access-Control-Request-Headers': 'Authorization'
  }
})
  .then(response => response.ok)
  .then(() => {
    // Send a GET request with the authorization header
    fetch('https://example.com', {
      method: 'GET',
      headers: {
        'Authorization': 'Bearer your_access_token'
      }
    })
      .then(response => response.json())
      .then(data => console.log(data))
      .catch(error => console.error(error));
  })
  .catch(error => console.error(error));
```
In this example, we first send a pre-flight request to the server with the `OPTIONS` method. We include the `Authorization` header with our access token, as well as the `Access-Control-Request-Method` and `Access-Control-Request-Headers` headers to indicate that we want to check if the server supports authorization headers.

If the server supports authorization headers, the server will respond with a `200 OK` status code and a `Access-Control-Allow-Methods` header that includes `GET`. We can then send a GET request with the authorization header to the server, and the server will respond with the data.

