Here's the R code that checks if all non-NA elements in a character vector can be converted to numeric:

```R
# Function to check if all non-NA elements in a character vector can be converted to numeric
is_numeric_vector <- function(x) {
  # Check if any element is NA
  if (any(is.na(x))) {
    return(FALSE)
  }

  # Try converting all elements to numeric
  tryCatch({
    x_numeric <- as.numeric(x)

    # Check if all elements were successfully converted
    if (all(!is.na(x_numeric))) {
      return(TRUE)
    } else {
      return(FALSE)
    }
  }, error = function(e) {
    return(FALSE)
  })
}

# Example usage
x <- c("1", "2", "3", "4", "5", "6")
y <- c("1", "2", "3", "4", "5", NA)
z <- c("1", "2", "3", "4", "5", "6", NA)

print(is_numeric_vector(x)) # Output: TRUE
print(is_numeric_vector(y)) # Output: FALSE
print(is_numeric_vector(z)) # Output: FALSE
```

Here's a decorator for Nest.js middleware that checks the `NODE_ENV` variable and calls `next()` if it's set to `true`, but returns `false` if it's set to `false`.

```typescript
import { Injectable, CanActivate, ExecutionContext } from '@nestjs/common';

@Injectable()
export class EnvironmentMiddleware implements CanActivate {
  canActivate(context: ExecutionContext): boolean {
    const request = context.switchToHttp().getRequest();
    const nodeEnv = request.nodeEnv;

    if (nodeEnv === 'production') {
      return false;
    }

    return true;
  }
}
```

To use this decorator, you can add it to your controller or route like this:

```typescript
import { Controller, Get, UseInterceptors } from '@nestjs/common';
import { ClassSerializerInterceptor } from '@nestjs/common';
import { EnvironmentMiddleware } from './environment.middleware';

@Controller('your-controller-path')
export class YourController {
  @Get()
  @UseInterceptors(ClassSerializerInterceptor)
  @UseInterceptors(EnvironmentMiddleware)
  getData(): any {
    // Your data fetching logic goes here
  }
}
```

In this example, the `EnvironmentMiddleware` is added as an interceptor to the `getData` method. The interceptor will check the `NODE_ENV` variable and return `false` if it's set to `false`, otherwise it will call `next()` and allow the request to proceed.

