To achieve this task using PowerShell, you can use the following script:

```powershell
# Define the function to check if an SID exists in the SIDHistory of any user or group in the domain
function CheckSIDInSIDHistory($sid) {
    $domain = (Get-ADDomain).Name
    $users = Get-ADUser -Filter {SID -eq $sid} -Properties SIDHistory
    $groups = Get-ADGroup -Filter {SID -eq $sid} -Properties SIDHistory

    if ($users -or $groups) {
        return True
    } else {
        return False
    }
}

# Define the function to recursively check folders for ACLs with SIDs in the SIDHistory
function CheckFoldersForSIDHistory($folderPath) {
    Get-ChildItem -Path $folderPath -Directory | ForEach-Object {
        $acl = Get-Acl -Path $_.FullName
        $sidsInSIDHistory = $acl.Access | Where-Object {$_.IdentityReference -match '^S-'} | Select-Object -ExpandProperty SID

        if ($sidsInSIDHistory -and ($sidsInSIDHistory | ForEach-Object { CheckSIDInSIDHistory($_) })) {
            Write-Host "Folder: $($_.FullName) - ACL contains SIDs in SIDHistory"
        }

        CheckFoldersForSIDHistory($_.FullName)
    }
}

# Call the function to check the root folder
CheckFoldersForSIDHistory("C:\")
```

This script defines two functions: `CheckSIDInSIDHistory` and `CheckFoldersForSIDHistory`. The first function checks if an SID exists in the SIDHistory of any user or group in the domain. The second function recursively checks folders for ACLs with SIDs in the SIDHistory.

The script starts by checking the root folder ("C:\"). You can modify the path to check other folders as needed.

Please note that this script requires administrative privileges to run. Make sure you have the necessary permissions before running it.
